{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And Jacob lived.</b> The reason his name is read as Jacob after HaShem said to him (Genesis 35:10) ״לֹא יִקָּרֵא״ etc., ״לֹא יַעֲקֹב יֵאָמֵר עוֹד שִׁמְךָ״ — I have already written in parashat Vayishlaḥ in the explanation of the verse ״לֹא יִקָּרֵא״ etc., that it is because of the name of his soul that is fixed in him, whose name is Jacob, as is known from their words (Berakhot 7a) which they expounded on the verse (Psalms 46:9) ״אֲשֶׁר שָׂם שַׁמּוֹת בָּאָרֶץ״, and since it still abides in him this name is not uprooted, and two names are called upon him." "<b>Rather,</b> we must investigate this: by what does HaShem choose at times to call him Jacob and at times Israel? It is true that regarding the congregation of Israel we find that the name changes for them according to their deeds — when they do the will of the Place (i.e., HaShem) He designates them with the name Israel, and conversely their name is called Jacob; and so it is alluded to in their words (Zohar Balak 210a). But this reason would not apply to Jacob, for his actions were straightforward continually." "<b>However,</b> since the intellectual branch that Jacob embodied to grasp — by means of which he is designated by the name Israel — is an aspect of supernal holiness, you will find that that blessed species rejects the aspect of worry and sorrow and lowliness, for it comes from the place of joy and rest and greatness. And it shall be for a sign between your eyes: the day of Shabbat for rest, which HaShem commanded the children of Israel — and this is for the reason of the additional soul. And since it comes from a supernal place, HaShem commands to banish grief and anger and to call the Shabbat עֹנֶג (delight). And the very same things are said of Jacob in his grasping of the supernal name. HaShem indicates that He commanded with him the supernal soul bearing this name of Israel and lodged it within him — but this is only so long as there is no sorrow and no branch of opposition whatsoever to joy and to the perfection of holiness and purity. And when this is lacking — ״וַיִּנָּפַשׁ״ — it departs from him like the departure of the additional soul of the day of Shabbat, and at that time he is not called Israel, for the bearer of the name has gone and is not present then, and his name is called Jacob. And that soul returns again by means of the preparation and arrangement of what is ready, like the return of the additional soul every Shabbat by means of the preparation of the holy day. And with this you will proceed confidently to understand the reason in every place where he is designated by the name Jacob: it is because of a cause — that there occurs there something from the aspect of sorrow and grief." "And I will register for you some of the places to illuminate for you, and this is the first. At the beginning of HaShem's good utterance in parashat Vayishlaḥ, He said to him ״שִׁמְךָ יַעֲקֹב״ — meaning, then his name would be Jacob because he was then in mourning for his mother, as per their words of blessed memory (Bereshit Rabbah chapter 82) who said that the statement ״וַיְבָרֶךְ אוֹתוֹ״ refers to the blessing of mourners, and for this reason his name was called Jacob. And therefore you will find that throughout that entire parashah HaShem called his name Jacob, and also at the time of Rachel's death his name was called Jacob, as it is written (Genesis 35:20) ״וַיַּצֵּב יַעֲקֹב מַצֵּבָה עַל קְבֻרָתָהּ״. And after the days of sorrow were completed, HaShem called his name Israel, as it is written (ibid. 21) ״וַיִּסַּע יִשְׂרָאֵל״, ״בִּשְׁכֹּן יִשְׂרָאֵל״. And although it says there ״וַיִּהְיוּ בְנֵי יַעֲקֹב״, you should know that when the sons are at a lesser level on account of their deeds, even though the father is designated by the name Israel, they are designated by the name Jacob. And in what is before us, on account of the deed that Reuben did, He called them by the name ״בְּנֵי יַעֲקֹב״. And what it further says there (Genesis 35:27) ״וַיָּבֹא יַעֲקֹב אֶל יִצְחָק אָבִיו״ — because he was in the presence of Isaac his father, Scripture did not treat him with the sovereignty and greatness alluded to in the name Israel. And so you will find that they said in Midrash Tehillim (Yalkut Shimoni 955), and this is its language: If Abraham had been alive, how could Isaac have exercised sovereignty? And likewise all the patriarchs and the righteous etc., end quote. You see that sovereignty withdraws in the presence of his father, and for this reason his name was called Jacob. And likewise, that He called him by the name Jacob in parashat Vayeshev — Scripture informs us that all the while Jacob was then in the land of Canaan he was in grief and sighing and at the level of the name Jacob, except for a few times which Scripture inscribed with the name Israel, as it says (Genesis 37:3) ״וְיִשְׂרָאֵל אָהַב״ etc. — then he was joyful and prepared without sorrow, to be called by the name Israel. And you will find that from the time Joseph was sold his name was not called Israel except three times: (Genesis 43:6) ״וַיֹּאמֶר יִשְׂרָאֵל לָמָּה הֲרֵעֹתֶם״ etc., ״וַיֹּאמֶר יְהוּדָה אֶל יִשְׂרָאֵל אָבִיו״, (Genesis 43:8) ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵיהֶם יִשְׂרָאֵל אֲבִיהֶם״ (Genesis 43:11), and these need to be expounded as speaking to them in the manner of sovereignty, on account of the necessity he saw that they did not take heed of his distress, and he exerted authority over them, hinting that he would not forgive them on account of being their father, and also that they would accept his words. And from the time he was informed about Joseph — ״וַתְּחִי רוּחַ יַעֲקֹב״ (Genesis 45:27) — and the spirit of HaShem rested upon him, Scripture immediately said ״וַיֹּאמֶר יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Genesis 45:28), ״וַיִּסַּע יִשְׂרָאֵל״, ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים לְיִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Genesis 46), until he was informed of the exile and accepted upon himself the grief, he reverted to being called Jacob — except at the time when Joseph was revealed to him, when because of the great joy HaShem designated him with the name Israel, as it is written (Genesis 46:29) ״וַיַּעַל לִקְרַאת יִשְׂרָאֵל אָבִיו״, ״וַיֹּאמֶר יִשְׂרָאֵל אָמוּתָה הַפָּעַם״ (Genesis 46:30). And from then onward he was not called by the name Israel all the days he was in the land of Egypt, until the day the time arrived for him to depart to his world. And what it says (Genesis 47) ״וַיֵּשֶׁב יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ refers to the collective of his sons, that the name Israel would be ascribed to them when they walk in the way of uprightness, and the power of the sons is greater than the power of their father, for the sons — whenever their ways improve — will be called by the name Israel because they are many, whereas Jacob is precise in the calling of his name to allude to the time of his state. And he was not called by the name Israel all the while he was in the land of Egypt, until the time drew near for him to die — then his name was called Israel, on account of the addition to their spirit, and this is the sod of the expansion of a person's soul at the time of his death (Zohar vol. 1, 218a). Therefore it says ״וַיִּקְרְבוּ יְמֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, ״וַיִּשְׁתַּחוּ יִשְׂרָאֵל״. And proceed according to this path to understand in every place that he is called by a certain name — it is his name, for a reason that you will understand there. And look at the words of the Zohar (Zohar vol. 1, 211a) and you will find support for our words. And all this is the way of peshat, and there are things within according to the spirituality of the matters, and the discerning one will understand." "<b>And Jacob lived in the land of Egypt etc.</b> One must know why it was necessary to state that he lived in the land of Egypt. And further it is difficult: it should have sufficed to say only ״וַיְהִי יְמֵי יַעֲקֹב״ etc. ״one hundred and forty-seven״ etc., and I know by calculation that he lived seventeen years in the land of Egypt, based on what was stated earlier in the notification in parashat Vayiggash, as it is written (Genesis 47:9) ״שְׁנֵי מְגוּרַי שְׁלֹשִׁים וּמְאַת שָׁנָה״. Furthermore, why was it necessary to say <b>the years of his life</b> — would it not have sufficed to say ״וַיְהִי יְמֵי יַעֲקֹב״?" "Or perhaps, because all the days of Jacob he had no rest and no tranquility — for from the moment he was born his adversary was born, and before his sun had even set Laban came and these added to his troubles, and after him there arose the distress of Dinah, and besides these came the fury of the loss of Joseph. This is what the verse means by ״וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם שְׁבַע עֶשְׂרֵה שָׁנָה״ — these were his life, and not the earlier years. And adjacent to this the verse says <b>וַיְהִי יְמֵי יַעֲקֹב שְׁנֵי חַיָּיו</b>, to allude that these were the days in which he had vitality. And the verse's saying ״וַיְהִי יְמֵי יַעֲקֹב״ — meaning those seventeen years — are שְׁנֵי חַיָּיו, meaning he had life in them. And the phrase ״יְמֵי יַעֲקֹב״ connects with what follows it: ״יְמֵי יַעֲקֹב וְגוֹ׳ שֶׁבַע״ and so on." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to the saying of the Sages of blessed memory (Tana devei Eliyahu Rabbah 5): whoever ends his days in goodness, it is as though all his days were in goodness. And this is the reason for (Job 8:7) ״וְהָיָה רֵאשִׁיתְךָ וְגוֹ׳ וְאַחֲרִיתְךָ יִשְׂגֶּה מְאֹד״, and likewise the verse says (Ecclesiastes 7:8) ״טוֹב אַחֲרִית דָּבָר״ and so on. And this is what the verse means by <b>וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב</b> — a life of tranquility <b>בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם</b>. In this it was considered in his eyes that the days of Jacob were שְׁנֵי חַיָּיו, meaning, years of life for all his days, which are שֶׁבַע שָׁנִים and so on. And the reason for the word <b>וַיְהִי</b> — which is an expression of distress — intends to say that even though his days were in distress, nevertheless they reverted in his eyes to be all days of life." "<b>Now there is</b> for us to investigate this: why did the verse change the order of the count of Jacob's years from the order followed in counting the years of the righteous, where it places the larger number before the smaller? Regarding Abraham it is written (Genesis 25:7) ״מְאַת שָׁנָה וְשִׁבְעִים שָׁנָה״ and so on; regarding Isaac it is written (Genesis 35:28) ״מְאַת שָׁנָה וּשְׁמֹנִים שָׁנָה״; regarding Sarah it is written (Genesis 23:1) ״מֵאָה שָׁנָה וְעֶשְׂרִים״ and so on. Or perhaps it is because it placed first the smaller count, which is the latter period, in which he had tranquility and a good life." "<b>Further,</b> the verse may intend in another manner, namely that the days during which Jacob's heart was whole and his desires complete throughout his life are seven and forty years. The reason is as they said of blessed memory, that he loved Rachel, and she was his destined mate whom Jacob chose for herself and for the children she would bear, as it says (Genesis 37:3) ״אָהַב אֶת יוֹסֵף מִכָּל״ and so on. And in this there is also an ancient sod. And let this be a sign for you: all the time that Joseph was not with him he placed sackcloth on his loins. And when you tally the days he was in the company of Rachel plus the years Joseph was with him, you will find forty-one years. For he spent twenty years in the house of Laban; subtract seven until he married Rachel, leaving thirteen. And within them are subsumed the years of Joseph, six years, for when he was born Jacob began tending Laban's flock, as it is written (Genesis 31:41) ״וְשֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים בְּצֹאנֶךָ״. And when they sold him he was seventeen years old; subtract six, leaving eleven, plus thirteen — that is twenty-four. And seventeen in Egypt — that is forty-one. And six years that Jacob spent with the younger children, which are days when the heart is joyful and empty of grief — that is forty-seven. And this is what the verse means by <b>וַיְהִי יְמֵי יַעֲקֹב שְׁנֵי חַיָּיו</b> — that he had life for שֶׁבַע שָׁנִים וְאַרְבָּעִים. And therefore he chose to place the smaller figure first, in order to indicate this." "<b>And the reason</b> for the word <b>וַיְהִי</b> according to this approach intends to indicate that our father Jacob did not live out the full measure of all his days. And this is as they said of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 100:9): because he said ״לֹא יִחְיֶה״ and thereby cut short the years of Rachel, his days were diminished from the count of the years of Isaac, which were one hundred and eighty, by thirty-three years — corresponding to the numerical value of ״יִחְיֶה״. Therefore the Torah alluded to the pain of the loss of the years of the righteous man through the word <b>וַיְהִי</b>." "<b>And there remains</b> to give a reason for why Scripture counts the years of Jacob before his time came to depart, for you see that after Scripture counted ״וַיְהִי יְמֵי יַעֲקֹב״ it said after it the matter of Joseph's oath, and afterward said ״וַיְהִי אַחֲרֵי הַדְּבָרִים וַיֹּאמֶר לְיוֹסֵף״ etc., and properly it should not have mentioned the tally of his days until the day of his death. Perhaps it intends to inform of a matter through which Jacob sensed that his days had drawn near to die, for on this account he called his son to Joseph etc. And it says <b>וַיְהִי יְמֵי יַעֲקֹב שְׁנֵי חַיָּיו</b> etc. — meaning, on account of what preceded him, namely the knowledge of the years of Isaac, which were one hundred and eighty years; and also because the righteous man sensed in his inadvertence the error he had made in the curse ״לֹא יִחְיֶה,״ and he knew its punishment, for the patriarchs were prophets and the holy spirit clothed them. Therefore when he calculated the years of his father and subtracted thirty-three from them, he feared the approach of his day and called his son etc.:" "<b>And by way of</b> allusion, the verse is explained as to why Elohim informed in the aspect of Joseph that he was the one who dispensed food, and that he sustained and kept alive a great multitude, as he said (Genesis 50:20) ״לְהַחֲיוֹת עַם רָב״ — and this aspect had been a debtor to its counterpart in the aspect of influence upon it. For we have learned that Jacob sustained and provided for Joseph seventeen years, as it is written (Genesis 37:2) ״בֶּן שְׁבַע עֶשְׂרֵה״ etc.; therefore he repaid this aspect and influenced upon Jacob in the measure of double-repayment many times over, multiplying the seventeen years — and this is what is alluded to: <b>וַיְחִי יַעֲקֹב בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם שְׁבַע עֶשְׂרֵה שָׁנָה.</b> And without this, it is possible that his days would have been complete earlier, but it was in order to repay Joseph the goodness of kindness he had received from him; and therefore the two are better than the one, for each one did good for the number of טו״ב years, and it was fitting to repay this one to that one. And you will find that they said, of blessed memory (Zohar Vayeshev 182b), that Joseph is in the aspect of vav of plenitude and vav, as is known to those who know the hidden things to HaShem our Elohim."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {3 "The Yalkut Shimoni reference תתע״ה is here rendered by its common association, Yalkut Shimoni 955; the exact numeration may vary by edition. The Zohar references folio numbers (רי״א, רי״ח) are transliterated from Hebrew gematria notation in the original.", 6 "The citation of Tana devei Eliyahu Rabbah is resolved here as the Sages of blessed memory; the chapter reference (ה) is retained as given.", 10 "The phrase 'שְׁנֵי אָבִיו' (the years of his father) is taken literally as referring to Isaac's lifespan of 180 years; Jacob subtracted his own age at that point (147) from Isaac's 180 to arrive at 33, which he connected to the decree implied by his curse.", 11 "The wordplay טו״ב / טובים rests on the gematria of טו״ב (17) coinciding with the word טוב ('good'); rendered literally to preserve the visible Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:51:04.643874153Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}